<h1>Intuitionism in Ethics</h1><div id="pubinfo"><em>First published Mon Jul 27, 2026</em></div>

<div id="preamble">

<!--pdf exclude begin-->

<p class="smaller">
[<em>Editor&rsquo;s Note: The following new entry by Matt Bedke
replaces the
 <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2026/entries/intuitionism-ethics/">former entry</a>
 on this topic by the previous author.</em>]</p>

<!--pdf exclude end-->

<p>
What would philosophical ethics be without intuitions? Consider the
following sample of cases and propositions, which are meant to elicit
an intuitive response.</p>

<dl class="sentag tag15em">
<dt>1.</dt>
<dd>

<dl class="sentag tag15em">
<dt>A.</dt>
<dd>A trolley is bearing down on five innocent people tied to the
track. There is a side track that only has one innocent person tied to
it. You are standing in front of a switch that will turn the trolley
onto the side track. If you do not flip the switch the trolley will
hit and kill the innocent five. If you flip the switch, the trolley
will hit and kill the innocent one. Intuitively, is it permissible to
flip the switch?</dd>
<dt>B.</dt>
<dd>A trolley is bearing down on five innocent people tied to the
track. You are standing on a footbridge over the trolley next to one
large man. If you do not push the large man onto the tracks, the
trolley will hit and kill the innocent five. If you push the large man
onto the tracks, the trolley will hit and kill him before coming to a
stop and the other five will live. Intuitively, is it permissible to
push the large man onto the tracks? (Thomson 1985).</dd>
</dl> </dd>
<dt>2.</dt>
<dd>

<dl class="sentag tag15em">
<dt>A.</dt>
<dd>You could donate $5 to charity to save one innocent child half way
around the world. Or you could decline to donate and go about your
day. Intuitively, is it permissible to decline to donate?</dd>
<dt>B.</dt>
<dd>You are walking through the park and you notice a child drowning
in a pond. If you decline to rescue the child will die. If you rescue
the child will live but it will cost you $5 to clean your shoes.
Intuitively, is it permissible to decline to rescue?</dd>
<dt>C.</dt>
<dd>Consider: the physical proximity of a potential beneficiary is not
directly morally relevant. Intuitively true? (Singer 1972).

<!--pdf include <br />
<br />
pdf include--></dd>
</dl> </dd>
<dt>3.</dt>
<dd>World W<sub>1</sub> has ten billion people all with a high quality
of life. Let the aggregate welfare in W<sub>1</sub> be <em>n</em>.
World W<sub>2</sub> has many, many more people all with lives barely
worth living. The aggregate welfare in W<sub>2</sub> is much greater
than <em>n</em>, for the lesser welfare of each person is more than
made up for by the sheer number of people. Intuitively, is
W<sub>2</sub> better than W<sub>1</sub>? (Parfit 1984: ch. 17).</dd>
<dt>4.</dt>
<dd>Consider: One has a <em>prima facie</em> duty to fulfill
one&rsquo;s promises (alternatively put, that one has promised to
<em>phi</em> is a (moral) reason to <em>phi</em>) (Ross 1930: ch.2).
Intuitively true?</dd>
<dt>5.</dt>
<dd>Consider: if <em>a</em> is better than <em>b</em>, and <em>b</em>
is better than <em>c</em>, then <em>a</em> is better than <em>c</em>.
Intuitively true?</dd>
</dl>

<p>
When people have intuitive responses, it is thought that the
intuitions provide some epistemic support for that which is intuitive.
But there are good questions about what intuitions are and what the
nature of the epistemic support is meant to be.</p>

<p>
To approach these issues, we begin with the history of Ethical
Intuitionism in
 <a href="#HistEthiIntu">section 1</a>.
 <a href="#SelfEvidProp">Section 2</a>
 discusses moral epistemologies that give a significant role to
self-evident moral propositions.
 <a href="#IntuSeem">Section 3</a>
 discusses moral epistemologies that identify intuitions as seeming
states that play a foundational epistemic role.
 <a href="#Appr">Section 4</a>
 discusses the role of intellectual apprehension in some intuitional
theories.
 <a href="#ConcComp">Section 5</a>
 discusses the role of conceptual competence.
 <a href="#OutpSyst1Proc">Section 6</a>
 discusses psychological theories that locate intuitions in fast
cognitive processes psychologists refer to system 1 processing.
 <a href="#SomeChal">Section 7</a>
 discusses a host of challenges to intuitions and their epistemic
roles.</p>
</div> 

