<h2 id="HistEthiIntu">1. The History of Ethical Intuitionism</h2>

<p>
There is a school of thought known as Ethical Intuitionism associated
with some figures in British moral philosophy beginning in the late
seventeenth century and up through the mid-twentieth century. Though
Ethical Intuitionism introduces and elaborates on some epistemic
views, it typically bundles those views with other commitments in
first-order ethics and moral metaphysics. In terms of first-order
ethics, it affirms a plurality of moral principles. In terms of moral
metaphysics, it affirms non-naturalism for moral properties and facts.
This section briefly highlights major themes of this school of
thought, touching on the work of Samuel Clarke, Richard Price, H. A.
Prichard, and W. D. Ross.</p>

<p>
Clarke wrote in the early eighteenth century, and he was largely
reacting to Hobbes&rsquo;s moral and political philosophy. Hobbes had
argued that our moral duties are the result of a social contract.
Clarke, on the other hand, thought there were moral duties that exist
prior to and independently of any contract (including the duty to
abide one&rsquo;s contracts), and these duties ultimately rest on
fundamental, necessary facts about which of our behaviors are fitting
and which of them unfitting (1706 [1969: &sect; 225]).</p>

<p>
For Clarke, there is more than one fundamental duty. There is a
fundamental duty of piety to God (1706 [1969: &sect; 240]), a
fundamental duty of equity and love toward others (1706 [1969: &sect;
241]), and a fundamental duty of self-preservation (1706 [1969: &sect;
245]). All other duties can be derived from these fundamental ones
(1706 [1969: &sect; 245]).</p>

<p>
In terms of epistemology, Clarke maintained that the fittingness and
unfittingness of these behaviours are &ldquo;self-evident&rdquo; (1706
[1969: &sect; 227]). Clarke also said that the behaviors that are
fitting or unfitting will <em>appear</em> to be so to the
<em>understandings</em> of all intelligent beings (1706 [1969: &sect;
230]). As we shall see, talk of self-evidence and how things appear to
the understanding are both central to discussions of contemporary
intuitionism.</p>

<p>
Let me next turn to Price, who was writing in the mid to late
eighteenth century. Whereas Clarke was largely responding to
Hobbes&rsquo; contractualism, Price was largely responding to the
sentimentalism of philosophers like Hutcheson and Hume, and it is
probably fair to characterize him as a realist about some moral
duties. That is, some moral duties are independent of our attitudes
and stances toward those actions that are our duties, and moral
judgments about these duties are not sourced in our sentiments or
other conative attitudes. Like Clarke, Price is a pluralist about
basic moral duties. He believed in duties to God, duties to ourselves,
duties of benevolence as well as duties of gratitude, veracity, and
justice (1758 [1969: &sect;&sect; 733&ndash;741]).</p>

<p>
According to Price, we come to know about fundamental moral duties via
<em>understanding</em>, which is taken to be a rational way of knowing
to be distinguished from the sentimentalists&rsquo; <em>moral
sense</em>. Moreover, Price uses the term &ldquo;intuition&rdquo;
(which Clarke did not as far as we know) and says that intuition is
the use of understanding to discern the nature of things (1758 [1969:
&sect; 674]).</p>

<p>
The simplicity of moral properties, such as rightness and goodness,
and our ideas of them, was important for many early intuitionists such
as Price. He accepted the empiricist doctrine that simple ideas must
be acquired by immediate intuition, as all simple ideas must &ldquo;be
ascribed to some power of <em>immediate</em> perception in the human
mind&rdquo; (1758 [1969: &sect; 672]), i.e., either sensibility or the
understanding. Consequently, the ideas of right and wrong must be
immediate perceptions of either sensibility or the understanding.</p>

<p>
If right and wrong are just feelings of approval or disapproval caused
in us by natural properties or objects, then the idea of right and
wrong will be given by our senses, for these ideas will be merely the
effect that the perception of certain things has on sensibility. If,
however, right and wrong are real properties of actions, then they
could not be apprehended by any empirical sense, for we have no such
sensation of right and wrong when we apprehend right or wrong actions.
Rather, what we &ldquo;see&rdquo; via the understanding is that these
actions are right, or wrong. This seeing is an immediate apprehension,
but it is intellectual rather than sensible.</p>

<p>
Price writes,</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
It is scarcely conceivable that anyone can impartially attend to the
nature of his own perceptions, and determine that, when he thinks
gratitude and beneficence to be <em>right</em>, he perceives nothing
<em>true</em> of them, and <em>understands</em> nothing, but only
receives an impression from <em>sense</em>. (Price 1758 [1969: &sect;
678])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Price concedes that certain feelings may attend our apprehension of
right and wrong, but these impressions are merely the consequence of
our perception of right and wrong. They are not what is perceived. For
Price, one approves of some act because one sees that it is right or
good.</p>

<p>
Running alongside these ideas about the rational apprehension of some
truths, Price also appeals to appearances in a way that resembles
Clarke&rsquo;s appeal to appearances:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Is there nothing truly wrong in the absolute and eternal misery of an
innocent being?&mdash;&ldquo;It <em>appears</em> wrong <em>to
us</em>&rdquo;.&mdash;And what reason can you have for doubting that
it <em>is</em> as it <em>appears</em>? (1758 [1969: &sect; 679])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Next, consider Prichard. Writing in the early and mid-twentieth
century, Prichard was largely responding to the consequentialism of
figures like Sidgwick and Moore. Sidgwick had characterized
intuitionism as the view that right conduct is conduct that conforms
to certain unconditionally binding principles (1907: book I, ch. 8),
to be contrasted with a consequentialist view where right conduct is
conduct that brings about good outcomes. Prichard wants to defend
intuitionism so understood, largely by criticizing consequentialism.
His considered positive view can be hard to discern, but he seems to
think one&rsquo;s moral obligations are grounded in (1) the nature of
the state of affairs produced, and (2) the relations one bears to
oneself or others (e.g., being a parent) (1912 [2002: 12]).</p>

<p>
In terms of epistemology, Prichard maintained that knowledge of our
obligations is immediate, and so not derived from premises about the
goodness of the outcomes to be produced. He draws a mathematical
analogy.</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
This apprehension [of a particular moral obligation] is immediate, in
precisely the sense in which a mathematical apprehension is immediate,
<em>e.g.</em>, the apprehension that this three-sided figure, in
virtue of its being three-sided, must have three angles. Both
apprehensions are immediate in the sense that in both insight into the
nature of the subject directly leads us to recognise its possession of
the predicate; and it is only stating this fact from the other side to
say that in both cases the fact apprehended is self-evident. (1912
[2002: 13])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
This brief historical overview concludes with the figure most
influential in contemporary debates: W. D. Ross. Ross wrote in the
early to mid-twentieth century as well, and he was largely criticizing
the consequentialism of G.E. Moore. Ross asked us to consider a right
action like keeping a promise, and ask what <em>makes</em> it right.
The consequentialist reply is that right actions are right because
they produce the most good. Ross rejoined that this is not what the
&ldquo;plain man&rdquo; really thinks. Instead, the plain man thinks
that what makes the action right is that one promised to do it (1930
[2002: 17&ndash;18]).</p>

<p>
Expanding on this thought, Ross thinks utilitarianism ignores morally
significant relations we bear to others by assuming that the only
relevant relation is that between benefactor and beneficiary. For him,
that one has promised to do something, for example, is morally
relevant in its own right. If it is, consequentialism is extensionally
and explanatorily inadequate. Suppose I promise to help someone move
apartments, but I can produce slightly more good by breaking that
promise and doing something else instead. Consequentialism says I
should break the promise, whereas Ross claims there are cases like
this where the duty of fidelity (as he calls it) is more weighty than
the duty to promote the good (a duty of beneficence) (1930 [2002:
17&ndash;18, 35]).</p>

<p>
Ross posited seven &ldquo;<em>prima facie</em>&rdquo;
duties&mdash;duties of (1) fidelity, (2) reparation, (3) gratitude,
(4) justice, (5) beneficence, (6) self-improvement, and (7)
non-maleficence (1930 [2002: 21]). Though he uses the term
&ldquo;<em>prima facie</em>&rdquo;, it is generally agreed that he
meant these are duties as far as they go, or <em>pro tanto</em> (1930
[2002: 19]).</p>

<p>
In terms of epistemology, these duties are self-evident just like
mathematical axioms or the validity of a form of inference is
self-evident (1930 [2002: 29]). Price claimed that self-evident truths
are &ldquo;incapable of proof&rdquo; (1758 [1969: &sect; 704]). At one
point Ross appears to agree, as he says self-evident moral
propositions &ldquo;cannot be proved, but &hellip; just as certainly
need no proof&rdquo; (1930 [2002: 30]). But elsewhere he makes only
the more restricted claim that such propositions do not <em>need</em>
any proof, which should be his considered view (1930 [2002: 29]). A
self-evident proposition is one that we <em>can</em> be justified in
believing without an argument, but this does not rule out the
possibility that there may be such an argument, or that the
proposition can be (justifiably) believed regardless of its
self-evident status, as might happen were we to believe them on the
basis of testimony (Audi 2004: 45).</p>

<p>
In some cases there will be more than one <em>pro tanto</em> duty
operative and the duties will conflict. For Ross, what one ought to do
all considered in such cases is not self-evident (which might conflict
with Prichard&rsquo;s view), nor can it be derived from the <em>pro
tanto</em> duties and their weights. To resolve conflicts of duties,
one must use judgment (1930 [2002: 30&ndash;31]).</p>

<p>
In addition to appealing to the idea of self-evidence, Ross also seems
to think that we apprehend the truth of the self-evident principles by
something like intuitive induction. That is, the epistemology starts
with particular cases where the general principles are operative, and
via those particular cases we come to see the truth of the generality
(1930 [2002: 33]). Moreover, he thinks that ethics is unlike science
in that it cannot appeal to sense experience for data in support of
one ethical theory or another. Instead, &ldquo;the moral convictions
of thoughtful and well-educated people are the data of ethics, just as
sense-perceptions are the data of natural science&rdquo; (1930 [2002:
41]).</p>

<p>
So much for quick historical notes. We can see that Ethical
Intuitionism is a family of views where the following features are
salient.</p>

<ol>

<li>A pluralist deontology&mdash;there are several basic moral duties
that are not analyzed in terms of promoting the good.</li>

<li>Realism about moral facts&mdash;some moral facts are independent
of the attitudes or stances of any actual or hypothetical beings (and
are likely non-natural or <em>sui generis</em>, too).</li>

<li>Cognitivism about moral judgments&mdash;moral judgments are
beliefs, not desire-like attitudes.</li>

<li>The self-evidence of some moral propositions.</li>

<li>The apparent (/seeming) truth of some moral propositions.</li>

<li>Non-inferential justification for some moral beliefs&mdash;for
some justified moral belief, <em>B</em>(<em>q</em>), the justification
is not based in the justification enjoyed by some other belief,
<em>B</em>(<em>p</em>), where one infers <em>q</em> from
<em>p</em>.</li>

<li>Rationalism for some moral knowledge&mdash;some moral facts can be
known <em>a priori</em> and through rational faculties, as opposed to
empirical or sentimental faculties.</li>
</ol>

<p>
The remainder of this entry focuses on (4)&ndash;(7), which are most
closely associated with intuitionism in ethics in contemporary
discussions. They most directly concern epistemology, which is
separable from the other ethical and metaethical views.</p>

<h2 id="SelfEvidProp">2. Self-Evident Propositions</h2>

<p>
The notion of a self-evident proposition is a term of art in
intuitionist thought, and it needs to be distinguished from certain
common-sense understandings with which it may easily be conflated. The
first thing to note is that a self-evident proposition is not the same
as an obvious truth. To begin with, obviousness is relative to certain
individuals or groups. What is obvious to you may not be obvious to
me. But self-evidence is not relative in this way. A proposition
cannot be self-evident to one person, but not to another. A
proposition is just self-evident, not self-evident <em>to</em>
someone. Secondly, there are many obvious truths that are not
self-evident. Certain well-known empirical truths, for example, that
if I drop a heavy object it will fall, or that the world is bigger
than a football, are obvious but not self-evident. There are also
self-evident propositions that may not be obvious to everyone, at
least prior to reflection, e.g., that if all <em>A</em>s are
<em>B</em>s and no <em>B</em>s are <em>C</em>s then no <em>C</em>s are
<em>A</em>s, or that one man can be the father and grandfather of the
same child.</p>

<p>
What then is it for a proposition to be self-evident? Locke says that
a self-evident proposition is one that &ldquo;carries its own light
and evidence with it, and needs no other proof: he that understands
the terms, assents to it for its own sake&rdquo; (1689: 1.3.4). Price
tells us that a self-evident proposition is immediate, and needs no
further proof, and goes on to say that self-evident propositions need
only be understood to gain assent (1758 [1969: &sect; 747]). Ross
writes that a self-evident proposition is &ldquo;evident without any
need of proof, or of evidence beyond itself&rdquo; (1930 [2002: 29]),
and Broad describes self-evident propositions as being</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
such that a rational being of sufficient insight and intelligence
could see it to be true by merely inspecting it and reflecting on its
terms and their mode of combination. (Broad 1936: 102&ndash;3)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
These passages may have led to the standard account of a self-evident
proposition in the contemporary literature. According to Robert Audi,
a self-evident proposition is &ldquo;a truth&rdquo; such that</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
an adequate understanding of it is sufficient both for being justified
in believing it and for knowing it if one believes it on the basis of
that understanding. (Audi 1998: 20; 1999: 206; 2004: 49; see also Audi
2008: 478).</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Similarly, Russ Shafer-Landau says</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
A proposition <em>p</em> is self-evident = if <em>p</em> is such that
adequately understanding and attentively considering just <em>p</em>
is sufficient to justify believing that <em>p</em>. (Shafer-Landau
2003: 247)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
It seems fairly clear that this is not going to be inferential
justification, for it is not as though understanding plays the role of
some premise from which belief in the self-evident proposition is
inferred.</p>

<p>
As for candidate self-evident moral propositions, many philosophers
have focused on certain general moral principles. W. D. Ross, as we
have seen, thought that there were seven self-evident moral duties,
such as the duty to keep one&rsquo;s promises. But others have
suggested that verdicts on cases can be self-evident (Clarke 1706:
192; Prichard 1912 [2002: 13]).</p>

<p>
One should distinguish knowing a self-evident proposition from knowing
that that proposition is self-evident. The former does not imply the
latter. Someone might know some self-evident proposition, such as that
if <em>A</em> is better than <em>B</em> and <em>B</em> is better than
<em>C</em> then <em>A</em> is better than <em>C</em>, but lack the
concept of self-evidence, so couldn&rsquo;t know that that proposition
is self-evident. One might even know a self-evident proposition whilst
endorsing a theory according to which no propositions are
self-evident.</p>

<p>
According to the standard account, a self-evident proposition is one
which an adequate understanding of the proposition justifies us in
believing it. That may sound odd. It is natural to think that
justified belief requires evidence for the proposition believed,
whereas adequately understanding a proposition never entails evidence
for the proposition. Robert Audi has, however, recently offered an
account of adequate understanding. He suggests that adequate
understanding is a kind of direct apprehension of the truth-makers for
the proposition, particularly concepts and relations that help make
the proposition true. This, he suggests, &ldquo;tends to yield an
intuition,&rdquo; which is the right sort of thing to justify belief
(Audi 2019: 379&ndash;380).</p>

<p>
Be that as it may, most recent intuitionists have shied away from
relying on the notion of a self-evident proposition. Before turning to
other themes, note that self-evident propositions have been
characterized in terms of their <em>epistemic role</em> when what we
ultimately want is some metaphysical account of self-evident
propositions capable of explaining that epistemic role. Not all true
propositions are such that adequate understanding justifies us in
believing them. If there is a special sub-class of propositions that
have these epistemic properties, plausibly they have them in virtue of
their non-epistemic properties. So what are those non-epistemic
properties? Some of the examples offered suggest an answer.</p>

<p>
Robert Audi says the following are self-evident (but not intuitive in
the sense of seeming true, at least not without reflection):</p>

<ol>

<li>the proposition that a grandmother can give birth to her
grandchild, and</li>

<li>the complex logical formula that if <em>p</em> entails <em>q</em>,
and <em>q</em> entails <em>r</em>, and <em>r</em> entails <em>s</em>,
and <em>s</em> is false, then <em>p</em> is false.</li>
</ol>

<p>
These seem like candidates for <em>analytic</em> propositions and
formulas, and it is plausible that the analyticity of a proposition
can help to explain how adequately understanding it justifies
believing it (Audi 2008: 478). However, most substantive moral
propositions offered as self-evident do not strike most as analytic.
Ross&rsquo;s <em>prima facie</em> duties, for example, appear to be
synthetic rather than analytic. And many synthetic moral propositions
are not self-evident. McGrath offers a nice example:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
[E]ven if the proposition that it is wrong to encourage children to
smoke is obvious to ordinary people in normal circumstances, it is not
a good candidate for a self-evident proposition. For a person might
understand the claim perfectly and yet not be justified in believing
it &hellip;. (2019: 145)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
If there are synthetic but self-evident moral propositions, it would
be nice to hear about the metaphysics of those propositions and
particularly the non-epistemic properties in virtue of which they are
self-evident. Further consideration would bring us into debates in
core analytic philosophy and questions like these: Given that there
are many synthetic propositions that are not knowable <em>a
priori</em>, what is it about some synthetic propositions, and what is
it about our powers of understanding, that makes them knowable <em>a
priori</em>? Can we explain this difference while retaining the
analytic-synthetic distinction, or is that something that has to
go?</p>

<p>
Self-evidence is not the only way to theorize about a priori moral
knowledge or justification. We now turn to an influential
alternative.</p>

<h2 id="IntuSeem">3. Intuitive Seemings</h2>

<p>
There are multiple kinds of mental states whereby some content can
seem true. The prototypical example is perception. Looking at a green
apple, it perceptually seems that this apple is green; looking at a
stick dipped in water, it perceptually seems that the stick is bent.
Another example is memory. Thinking back, I seem to remember locking
the door to the car; I seem to remember my grandfather&rsquo;s
birthday. Another example is proprioceptive. Closing my eyes, I have
some sense of where my limbs are. In general epistemology we see the
suggestion that intuitions are just another kind of seeming state, or
appearance state, distinct from these others (Bealer 1998; Pust 2000;
Huemer 2006; Chudnoff 2011a, 2013, 2020; Bengson 2015). And in moral
epistemology intuitionism is often associated with the idea that
ethical intuitions are just intuitive seemings where the content that
seems true is ethical content.</p>

<p>
Just as perceptual, memorial, and proprioceptive seemings are
fallible, so too intuitions qua seeming states are fallible. It can
seem to you that <em>p</em> when <em>p</em> is not the case. And just
as perceptual, memorial, and proprioceptive seemings are not beliefs,
so too intuitions are not beliefs. You can have an intuition that
<em>p</em> while failing to believe that <em>p</em>. Similarly, we can
plausibly distinguish intuitions from hunches, guesses, blind
impulses, vague sentiments, preferences for action, or familiar
opinion (Sidgwick 1907 [1981]: 211&ndash;212). Some have suggested
that intuitions are merely inclinations to believe (Williamson 2000;
Sosa 2007; Earlenbaugh &amp; Molyneux 2009). But the more common view
is that, though intuitions might provide some categorical basis for a
disposition to believe what is intuitive, we can distinguish
intuitions from mere inclinations to believe. One can be inclined to
belief without anything seeming to be the case in an intuitive
way.</p>

<p>
It is natural to wonder if there is some relation between the idea
that some propositions are self-evident and seeming state theory. One
thing to note is that seeming state theory is not necessarily an
alternative to self-evidence theory. For it could be that when one
adequately understands a self-evident proposition this manifests as a
seeming state. McGrath suggests an alternative way to blend the two
theories. She suggest that we characterize the self-evident in terms
of seemings:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
The self-evident propositions are simply those propositions&mdash;if
any&mdash;whose intellectually seeming true to a subject can be
correctly explained by their being true. (McGrath 2019: 148)</p>
</blockquote>

<h3 id="EpisIntuSeem">3.1 The Epistemology of Intuitive Seemings</h3>

<p>
Epistemically, the idea is that all these seeming states, intuitions
included, play an important epistemic role. We can follow
Huemer&rsquo;s nomenclature and call the epistemic principle
Phenomenal Conservatism: For all <em>p</em>, if it seems to you that
<em>p</em>, and if you believe that <em>p</em> based on it seeming to
you that <em>p</em>, your belief is <em>pro tanto</em>, defeasibly
justified (cf. Chudnoff 2011b; Huemer 2006; Pryor 2000). This basing
relation is not typically considered an inferential relation. In fact,
this epistemic role is well suited to an internalist, foundationalist
epistemology where seeming states are unjustified justifiers. Beliefs
based on them are foundationally justified and from that foundation we
can infer other beliefs that thereby enjoy inferential
justification.</p>

<p>
As articulated, Phenomenal Conservatism allows intuitions to justify
beliefs without any externalist conditions, such as a reliable
connection to the moral facts. To see this, imagine that we are being
massively deceived by an evil demon (unbeknownst to us) and none of
our seeming states are veridical. Still, according to the principle,
we are justified in believing that things are as they seem to be. A
more externalist epistemology would pay more attention to the right
connections with the external facts, and it would not allow beliefs to
be justified unless they had some good connection with the facts. For
example, we might not want a belief to be justified unless it was
produced by a reliable belief-forming process, in which case we want
beliefs based on intuitions to be part of a reliable belief-forming
process (Markie 2005: 356&ndash;357). And there is nothing to prevent
an epistemology of seemings that assigns some role to external
connections. Shafer-Landau (2003: chs. 11&ndash;12), for example, has
a moral epistemology that mixes internalist and externalist
elements.</p>

<p>
If we stick with Phenomenal Conservatism and its internalism, note
that it underwrites a standard practice in ethics where intuitions
play a central role in getting our beliefs off the ground, but best
explanations, reflective equilibrium, and coherence can ultimately
lead us to reject some things that were initially intuitive. Suppose
we have a bunch of moral intuitions: an intuition that P, and
intuition that Q,&hellip; and so on. On this basis we believe, and
have some defeasible justification for believing, that P, that
Q,&hellip; and so on. We can then consider what plausible theory best
explains any pattern that emerges from P, Q, etc. The outcome of this
process might be to ultimately reject some of P, Q, etc. But the main
idea here is that intuitions legitimately feed into a respectable form
of inquiry that gives intuitions some <em>pro tanto</em> and
defeasible epistemic weight, much like other seeming states. And much
like other seeming states, we might come to disbelieve some of the
things that seem true, based on our best overall theories (as we
reject the proposition that the stick in water is bent even though it
appears so.)</p>

<p>
Seen in this light, moral theory that relies on moral intuitions is
just one domain among many where some things seem true, one thereby
has some justification for believing that which seems true, and theory
helps to best explain all that is true. This resonates with
Ross&rsquo;s position. Recall that he said &ldquo;the moral
convictions of thoughtful and well-educated people are the data of
ethics, just as sense-perceptions are the data of natural
science&rdquo; (1930 [2002: 41]). Seeming state theorists simply think
seeming states play this role, not the convictions of thoughtful and
well-educated people.</p>

<h3 id="InteVsSentSeem">3.2 Intellectual vs Sentimental Seemings</h3>

<p>
So far, the seemings at issue have been characterized as intuitive
seemings. But virtually everyone who characterizes intuitions as
seeming states call them <em>intellectual</em> seemings or appearances
(see, e.g., Bealer 1998; Huemer 2005: ch. 5; Bengson 2015). If ethical
intuitions are intellectual seemings, this highlights continuity with
the rationalist tradition found in Ethical Intuitionism.</p>

<p>
But there are those who maintain that some ethical intuitions are
<em>sentimental</em> seemings. Sabine Roeser, for example, defends
what she calls <em>affectual intuitionism</em>, which is the view that
moral emotions are felt value judgments that provide epistemic access
to objective moral truths (2011: ch. 5). To be clear, though she uses
the term &ldquo;intuitionism&rdquo;, she does not think that ethical
intuitions are non-doxastic states. For her, moral emotions are partly
constituted by moral judgments, which provide epistemic access to
moral truths by reliably tracking the moral facts. For this reason, it
is not clear how she might distinguish intuitions from other sorts of
states, as the seeming state theory tries to do. Christine Tappolet
has a similar view, though she denies that having an emotion entails
having an evaluative judgment. Still, she thinks emotions track and
represent values in some way, and emotions are epistemically
indispensable to discovering evaluative facts (Tappolet 2011). Indeed,
there are many neo-sentimentalists who think moral judgments
&ldquo;come from&rdquo; sentiment in some way, but who do not
explicitly talk about intuitions as distinct from moral judgments.</p>

<p>
A neo-sentimentalist view that more clearly fits with seeming state
theory is that of Antti Kauppinen. For him, substantive moral emotions
are quasi-perceptual: they present evaluative content (e.g.,
indignation presents injustice). These emotional appearances are
<em>prima facie</em> justifiers, just like any other appearance. But
Kauppinen argues that grounding the seemings in emotion instead of the
intellect does a better job explaining two key features that
distinguish substantive moral intuitions from intuitions of other
sorts:</p>

<ol>

<li>their phenomenology is distinctive and diverse, often involving an
affective tone and</li>

<li>they provide (intrinsic) motivation (2013; see also Dancy
2014).</li>
</ol>

<p>
On the differences between moral intuitions and intuitions of other
sorts see also DePaul (2009: 211), Wielenberg (2014: 111), Bedke
(2008, 2016).</p>

<p>
Similarly, Eric Hutton (2022) defends a view he calls &ldquo;emotional
intuitionism&rdquo;. As with Roeser and Tappolet, it is not clear that
intuition <em>per se</em> plays a role. His main claim is that moral
<em>experiences</em> consist of emotions. He argues that this best
explains two observations:</p>

<ol>

<li>in the most compelling cases of moral experience, emotions are
present, and</li>

<li>the moral experience covaries with the presence of the
emotions.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Insofar as moral experiences consist of moral seemings, this would
classify moral intuitions and sentimental seemings.</p>

<p>
There is the possibility of pluralism about intuitive seemings in
ethics, where some are intellectual and others are sentimental.
Intuitions about formal matters like the transitivity of value, for
example, are not likely sentimental even if some substantive moral
intuitions are sentimental. And substantive intuitions about the moral
statuses of actions, perhaps the impermissibility of rape, are
arguably better candidates for sentimental seemings.</p>

<h2 id="Appr">4. Apprehension</h2>

<p>
Some intuitionists embrace an ability to rationally <em>apprehend</em>
certain facts. We find this idea both in those who are friendly to
self-evident propositions and those who are friendly to seeming state
theory. Unlike seeming states, apprehensions are factive. There are
different ways to develop this thought. You might think some
propositions are true thanks to the relations of the
properties/universals featured in the proposition. If so, you might
posit an intellectual ability to grasp these properties/universals and
relations among them (Huemer 2005: 124&ndash;126; see also Russell
1912: chs. IX&ndash;XI; Cuneo &amp; Shafer-Landau 2014). Similarly,
Bengson, Cuneo and Schafer-Landau (2023; 2024) endorse Price&rsquo;s
view that we can intellectually apprehend the <em>essences</em> of
certain act types. According to them, we can thereby intellectually
see (via what they call &ldquo;Pricean reflection&rdquo;) that certain
act types are essentially morally valanced (2024: ch. 4). For example,
we can intellectually see that</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
[I]t is essential to protecting one&rsquo;s children from lethal
danger that an action instantiates this property only if there is
strong moral reason to perform it. (2024: 340).</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
These theories of intellectual apprehension might help flesh out the
idea of a self-evident proposition. As noted above, we would like to
know what it is about some true propositions, or what it is about our
understanding, such that those propositions can be justifiably
believed or known on a basis of understanding them. We could start an
answer along the following lines. First, some propositions are true
thanks to the relations of the properties/universals featured therein.
Second, our powers of understanding are such that we can grasp the
relations between certain properties/universals, and when we do so it
is a kind of apprehension.</p>

<p>
For those who embrace intuitions as intellectual seemings and also
maintain that sometimes we intellectually apprehend certain moral
facts, there is a nice parallel with perceptual seemings and
perception. We can divide perceptual seemings into the good cases and
the bad cases. In good cases it not only perceptually seems that
<em>p</em>, but we perceive that <em>p</em>. In the bad cases, it only
perceptually seems that <em>p</em>, and we do not perceive that
<em>p</em>. From the inside, these might be indistinguishable and it
might be that the seemings in both cases provide justification, but
only in the good cases do we get knowledge. The same can be said for
intellectual seemings. Intellectual seemings always justify but only
in the good cases&mdash;the cases that are also intellectual
apprehensions&mdash;do we get knowledge.</p>

<h2 id="ConcComp">5. Conceptual Competence</h2>

<p>
Intuitions are often associated with conceptual competence in roughly
the following way. When one has a certain degree of conceptual
competence one possesses, perhaps implicitly and in ways that are
difficult to capture in language, criteria for what it takes to fall
under the concept (or what it takes to fall outside the concept). It
is then possible to craft cases and propositions in such a way that
they directly engage one&rsquo;s conceptual competence, and enable one
to apply a concept or concepts solely on the basis of conceptual
competence. For some views sympathetic to this line of thought see,
e.g., Goldman (2007: 4; 2013: passim), Graham and Horgan (1994), and
Ludwig (2007).</p>

<p>
Applied to the ethical domain, the thought is that some case-based
intuitions just are states of categorizing with a concept based solely
on conceptual competence (or this plus reflection), and some
intuitions concerning more general propositions are recognitions of
conceptual connections based solely on one&rsquo;s conceptual
competence (or this plus reflection).</p>

<p>
We could psychologize concept possession and competence, whereby
having a concept is to psychologically encode some criteria for how to
apply it. Or we could make the view less psychologized. For this
second option, we could connect it back with the apprehension account
above, and with a capacity of our understanding to apprehend abstract
relations between properties/universals. One such view would say that
what it is to fully grasp a concept is to have the capacity, if
favorable conditions, to apprehend those abstract relations.</p>

<p>
Robert Audi appears to endorse a view like this, though he makes use
of the idea of conceptual containment. He suggests that a self-evident
proposition is one where the concept of the subject <em>contains</em>
the concept of the predicate (Audi 2008: 479). Conceptual containment
is typically associated with analytic propositions, where one tries to
explain what it is for a proposition to be true in virtue of the
meanings of its constituents. Thus, &ldquo;a vixen is a female&rdquo;
is analytically true (or so the proposal goes) because the concept of
being a vixen contains the concept of being a female.</p>

<p>
Audi suggests, however, that not all cases of containment are
associated with analytic propositions. In support of this idea, Audi
proposes the following example: that burning a person with a red-hot
poker would cause him excruciating pain is a moral reason not to do it
(Audi 2008: 479). Here the concept of being a moral reason for action
allegedly contains the concept of avoiding the causing of pain in a
way that makes the second a sufficient condition for the first. It
takes grasping these concepts and some reflection to see the
correlative fact: that phi avoids the causing of pain is a reason to
phi.</p>

<p>
Similarly, Robert Cowan (2017) has suggested that one way to develop
Ross&rsquo;s view of self-evident <em>prima facie</em> duties is to
make those duties implicit commitments for anyone who possesses the
relevant moral concept. Adequate understanding, on this view, enables
one to make explicit conceptual connections (perhaps with the help of
reflection), and the Rossian duties are propositions that make
explicit those conceptual connections.</p>

<p>
The main problem with tying one&rsquo;s moral metaphysics and
epistemology too closely to conceptual competence is that it seems to
over-generate cases of conceptual incompetence. Start with your
favorite intuition that survives scrutiny and which you use to form a
belief. Is it not possible for someone to disagree with you even
though they possess the relevant concepts (to a very high degree),
they reflect adequately, and they are not otherwise cognitively
impaired? They might be mistaken in their judgment, but it does not
seem to be a <em>conceptual</em> mistake akin to the mistake one makes
when they deny that a vixen is a female (see Bedke 2008, 2010,
2016).</p>

<h2 id="OutpSyst1Proc">6. Outputs of System 1 Processing</h2>

<p>
It is worth mentioning a theory of intuitions more prevalent in the
psychology literature. It starts with the idea that cognition has two
reasoning processes, system 1 and system 2. System 1 processing is
characterized as implicit, fast, automatic, low effort, and modular
(think of gut reactions based on stereotypes and heuristics), while
system 2 processing is explicit, slow, deliberative, effortful, and
general-purpose (think of deductive reasoning with many premises).
Drawing on this distinction in cognitive science, some psychologists
working on moral cognition have suggested that some intuitions are
affective responses from system 1 processing (Haidt 2001; Haidt &amp;
Bjorklund 2007). Based on fMRI scans of brain activation, Joshua
Greene (2008) has argued that consequentialist judgments are largely
the products of system 2 while deontologist judgments are largely the
products of system 1.</p>

<p>
This approach is not necessarily inconsistent with other approaches.
But without any finer grained distinctions, it would lump intuitions
together with all sorts of other judgments that do not have the same
surface features or the same epistemic role. Hunches, impulses, and
other states whereby there is no proposition that seems true, and
which do not defeasibly justify, are likely some of the outputs of
system 1.</p>

<h2 id="SomeChal">7. Some Challenges</h2>

<h3 id="Disa">7.1 Disagreement</h3>

<p>
Many philosophers think that pervasive moral disagreement casts doubt
on the claim that certain moral propositions are self-evident. If
there were certain moral propositions that can be known if adequately
understood, then people with an adequate understanding of them would
believe them, and there would be universal assent amongst mature,
comprehending people. But there is no such universal assent, at least
for the substantive ethical propositions that intuitionists have used
to craft a moral theory. So none of those principles are self-evident.
Or so one can argue.</p>

<p>
One way intuitionists could defend the view is to grant disagreement
over allegedly self-evident propositions and suggest ways to deal with
the problem. This was Sidgwick&rsquo;s strategy. He thought that if
there was significant disagreement about the truth of some apparently
self-evident moral proposition, then that proposition might not be
self-evident. To deal with it, he gives guidance on how to do the best
we can in discerning what is self-evident. To guide us, he sets forth
four conditions sufficient to establish &ldquo;a significant
proposition, apparently self-evident, in the highest degree of
certainty attainable&rdquo; (1907: 338):</p>

<ol>

<li>the target proposition must be clear and precise,</li>

<li>its self-evidence must be ascertained by careful reflection,</li>

<li>taken as a whole, propositions taken as self-evident must be
mutually consistent, and</li>

<li>the target proposition must lack universal or general denial by
others, or at least lack denial among experts (Sidgwick 1907 [1981]:
211&ndash;212, 338&ndash;342).</li>
</ol>

<p>
He also warned that it is easy to confuse intuitions with blind
impulses, vague sentiments, preferences for action, conclusions from
rapid half-conscious processing, and current opinions to which
familiarity has given an illusory air of self-evidence, such as
conventional law (Sidgwick 1907 [1981]: 211&ndash;12). His own
attempts to be mindful of these considerations led him to propose the
following as self-evident: that the good of any one person is as
important as the good of any other, and that one is rationally bound
to aim at the good generally, not any part of it (Sidgwick 1907
[1981]: 382). From these and supplementary claims about the good, he
argues for a form of consequentialism called <em>Hedonic
Utilitarianism</em>.</p>

<p>
Other intuitionists allow that various other factors can lead to
disagreement over what is self-evident. Clarke, for instance, allowed
that stupidity, corruption, or perverseness may make one doubt
self-evident propositions (1706 [1969: &sect; 227]). John Balguy also
acknowledges that self-evident moral principles, like many other plain
and evident truths, may be, and have been, doubted, &ldquo;even by
philosophers and men of letters&rdquo; whom, presumably, he did not
regard as stupid or corrupt (1728 [1969: &sect; 458]). And Price
maintained that all forms of knowledge, including intuitive knowledge,
may be evident in different degrees (1758 [1969: &sect; 706]).
Intuition may be clear and perfect, but may sometimes be faint and
obscure. Such variance in degrees of clarity allows that a
self-evident proposition may be imperfectly and obscurely grasped, and
this may lead someone to deny its truth. Similarly, Moore claimed that
&ldquo;every way in which it is possible to cognise a true
proposition, it is also possible to grasp a false one&rdquo; (1903
[1993: 36]), and Ross notes that self-evident propositions may only be
evident to us once we reach a certain moral maturity (1930 [2002:
29]). Given all these ways in which the truth of a self-evident
proposition may be missed, it is no surprise that there is no
universal assent. But the absence of universal assent is quite
consistent with self-evidence, as long as one does not regard
&ldquo;self-evidence&rdquo; to mean, or imply, obviousness.</p>

<p>
Despite what has been said above, critics of intuitionism can claim
that the fact that there is fundamental disagreement between moral
philosophers and even between intuitionists themselves undermines the
view that certain propositions are self-evident. These philosophers
will have thought long and hard about the relevant propositions, and
(we would hope) have a very clear understanding of them. One would,
therefore, expect that if there were certain moral propositions the
truth of which could be apprehended by understanding, then moral
philosophers would converge on these truths. Persistent disagreement
amongst reflective, thoughtful, and comprehending moral philosophers
may cast doubt on the view that any of these propositions are
self-evident.</p>

<p>
An alternative strategy for intuitionists is to downplay the amount of
fundamental disagreement over allegedly self-evident propositions.
They might claim that a lot of moral disagreement stems from
disagreement about non-moral facts, such as what the consequences of a
certain act will be. For instance, two people might disagree about
whether it is permissible to boil lobsters alive just because they
disagree about whether lobsters can feel pain. Since the basis of
their moral disagreement is this disagreement about the relevant
neurological fact, if they agreed on this non-moral fact, we could
expect them to agree about the permissibility of boiling lobsters
alive.</p>

<p>
Furthermore, although people might disagree about the permissibility
of boiling lobsters alive, we may assume that they agree that pain is
a bad thing, and the infliction of undeserved pain is <em>prima
facie</em> wrong. If this assumption is correct, then the disputants
agree about the moral facts here. They disagree only about the
empirical, non-moral facts.</p>

<p>
Another factor that might explain moral disagreement is disagreement
about the strength of certain moral reasons. Many disputants might
agree about the non-moral facts, and about what is morally relevant,
but disagree about the weight that should be given to the different
moral considerations. So, for example, two people might disagree about
whether they ought to push a large man onto the rail tracks to derail
a trolley that would otherwise kill five people, but this is quite
consistent with them agreeing that the fact that this act would save
five people counts in favour of pushing the man off the bridge, and
that the fact that he would die if this were done counts against doing
it. In such a case there is agreement about what is relevant, and how
it is relevant, but disagreement about the weight of the competing
moral considerations&mdash;one person regards the evil of killing one
person as weightier than the good of saving five, while the other
regards the evil of killing one as outweighed by the good of saving
the five. There is still moral disagreement here, but it is simply a
difference in judgement about the application of agreed moral
principles.</p>

<p>
This fits with the intuitionist view held, e.g., by Ross, that causing
good outcomes is <em>prima facie</em> right, and that causing harm is
<em>prima facie</em> wrong, for Ross held that both of these
propositions are self-evident. He denied, however, that the
stringency, or weight, of these different <em>prima facie</em> duties
is self-evident (1939: 188).</p>

<p>
So far we have only discussed disagreement that concerns self-evident
propositions. Turning to the idea that intuitions are seemings, one
question is the extent to which different people have different
intuitive seemings when considering the same proposition or case. It
is worth emphasizing here that disagreement in moral judgment does not
imply that people have different intuitive seemings. Two people could
start with the same intuitions on cases. Reflection and lots of other
influences could lead one to embrace consequentialism and the other to
embrace deontology.</p>

<p>
Still, Michael Huemer (2008) has claimed that some ethical intuitions
are not shared, and he suggests this is due to a number of reasons:
that they have been influenced by one&rsquo;s culture, that they have
been influenced by evolutionary pressures, that they sometimes support
theories that promote one&rsquo;s self- interests, that they sometimes
line up with strong emotion, and that the abstract ones are prone to
an overgeneralization bias. On these grounds he is inclined to place
more trust in formal intuitions, like the intuition that if <em>a</em>
is better than <em>b</em> and <em>b</em> is better than <em>c</em>,
then <em>a</em> is better than <em>c</em> (the transitivity of value),
for he thinks such intuitions are less susceptible to the distorting
influences.</p>

<p>
Stich and Machery (2023) have compiled a helpful chart of some
experiments that purport to find differences in intuitions that
correlate with various demographic categories (e.g., religion, age,
gender, social class, etc.). To evaluate the significance of the
experiments, first note that it is difficult to experimentally measure
what intuitions people have. They can self-report, but it is not clear
the extent to which reports tap into intuitions as opposed to beliefs
or other mental states that are not intuitions <em>per se</em>
(Bengson 2013). As lately noted, it is easy for there to be a mismatch
between our intuitions and our judgments. Indeed, it is often quite
reasonable to fail to believe what one finds intuitive, just as it is
often reasonable to fail to believe what perceptually seems to be the
case. Consider someone who is familiar with the M&uuml;ller-Lyer
illusion, where one line can look longer than another, but one
reasonably believes it is not longer. Similarly, it is plausible to
suppose that many act consequentialists still have the intuition that
it is wrong to harvest organs from a healthy but non-consenting donor
to save five other lives. But because they have persuaded themselves
of the truth of act consequentialism, they would not believe this act
is wrong.</p>

<p>
Be that as it may, suppose there are demographic differences in what
people find intuitive. Though it is commonly supposed that this
somehow undermines the epistemic role of intuitions, nothing here
undermines the basic idea that intuitions provide pro-tanto,
<em>defeasible</em> justification for believing that which is
intuitive. Whether inter-personal differences in intuitions provide a
defeater is another question. Sinnott-Armstrong suggests that relying
on intuitions that vary between persons is akin to reliance on the
reading of one random thermometer in a box of one hundred
thermometers, some portion of which are known to be unreliable (2006b:
201). Bedke (2013) argues that this overstates the case. Drawing an
analogy with cases where others report <em>perceptual</em> seemings
that differ from your own, it is not always the case that reported
differences in seemings defeat the justification provided by
one&rsquo;s seeming states.</p>

<p>
Some of this literature taps into the broader issue of peer
disagreement, where the rough idea is that not any kind of
disagreement undermines your justification, but disagreement with an
epistemic peer could. It is not always clear that differences in
intuitive seemings are differences among peers, and indeed the very
fact that another does not share your intuitions might be some
evidence that they are not a peer (Audi 2008: 490; Enoch 2010:
979&ndash;981).</p>

<h3 id="FramOrdeEffe">7.2 Framing and Ordering Effects</h3>

<p>
Consider the following three cases:</p>

<ul class="hanging">

<li><strong>Switch:</strong> there are five people on the rail track,
and an out of control trolley that will kill all five of them. There
is a lever that would divert the trolley onto a different track. But
there is a single person on that track who would be killed if you pull
the lever and divert the trolley.</li>

<li><strong>Bridge:</strong> there are five people on the rail track,
and an out of control trolley that will kill all five of them. There
is a large man standing on the bridge over the track. If you pushed
him off the bridge onto the track, he would be killed. But he would
derail the trolley and so the five people on the track would be
saved.</li>

<li><strong>Trap door:</strong> there are five people on the rail
track, and an out of control trolley that will kill all five of them.
There is a man standing on the bridge over the track. If he fell onto
the track he would be killed, but would derail the trolley thus saving
the five people on the track. He is standing on a trap door that would
open and drop him onto the track if you pulled a lever.</li>
</ul>

<p>
People tend to say that they should pull the lever in Switch, but that
they ought not to push the man off the bridge in Bridge. Bridge looks
similar to Switch in that you would be killing one person to save
five. So why the different intuitions? One could try to explain away
these apparently conflicting intuitions by providing principles that
identify the morally relevant differences between the cases. That is
grist for the deontologist&rsquo;s mill.</p>

<p>
It would be troubling if the intuitions on these cases were not
stable, and particularly if they could be pushed around by morally
irrelevant influences. One does not want to base one&rsquo;s beliefs
on unreliable intuitions. Some experiments have tried to address the
issue. In Petrinovich and O&rsquo;Neill (1996) participants were given
trolley-type cases where the framing of the case was manipulated. If
given an option couched in terms of &ldquo;kill&rdquo; wording (e.g.,
&ldquo;to throw the switch which will result in the death of the one
innocent person&rdquo;), participants on average disagree slightly
with the action, whereas if given an option in terms of
&ldquo;save&rdquo; wording (e.g., &ldquo;to throw the switch which
will result in the five innocent people on the main track being
saved&rdquo;), subjects on average slightly agree with the action.</p>

<p>
That same paper reported other studies that showed ordering effects.
Subjects were asked to indicate their approval of actions (described
in terms of saving) in three trolley-type questions given in sequence.
One set of subjects got the questions in one sequence, and another set
of subjects got the same questions in reverse sequence. On average,
subjects more strongly approved of an action when it appeared first in
the sequence than when it appeared last, and the degree of approval
for the middle case varied depending on which of the other two
trolley-type cases came first.</p>

<p>
From framing effects like these, Sinnott-Armstrong argues that it is
reasonable to &ldquo;assign a large probability of error to moral
intuitions in general&rdquo; (Sinnott-Armstrong 2008: 99). For him,
any given moral intuition must be shown to be immune from such effects
before it can be justified.</p>

<p>
But it is not clear that this is the conclusion to draw. For one, it
is not clear these experiments tapped intuitions. The studies ask
participants to register their <em>approval of action</em> rather than
to classify actions under moral categories (e.g., impermissible,
permissible). So it is not clear that the findings reflect the
apparent truth of moral propositions. This is a difficult problem to
overcome. Other studies have asked for people&rsquo;s
<em>judgments</em> about what is obligatory or permissible on
hypothetical cases (Tobia, Buckwalter &amp; Stich 2013; Schwitzgebel
&amp; Cushman 2012; Lombrozo 2009), but as we noted above
people&rsquo;s judgments do not necessarily reflect their intuitions.
Still, it might be that judgments show order effects for both
philosophers and non-philosophers (Schwitzgebel &amp; Cushman 2012;
see also Lombrozo 2009). Tobia, Buckwalter &amp; Stich (2013) reported
an &ldquo;actor-observer&rdquo; bias for both philosophers and
non-philosophers, where judgments differ depending on whether the
experimental participant or some other party is cast as the actor
(e.g., the one considering slipping the switch). But there are two
other studies where that failed to replicate (Tobia, Chapman, &amp;
Stich 2013; Horvath &amp; Wiegmann 2022). For a recent set of studies
showing some framing effects and biases among philosophers and
non-philosophers see Horvath and Wiegmann (2022).</p>

<p>
Second, it is worth noting that in some of these studies what is
measured is the degree to which participants agree with certain
claims, or the degree to which certain actions are bad or good.
Statistically significant differences in such things is not
necessarily a signal of the unreliability of the judgment. One could
reliably approve or reliably judge something good, but unreliably
approve to the same degree, or say it is good to the same degree,
across morally irrelevant manipulations.</p>

<p>
Third, even if we grant that there are framing and ordering effects
for intuitive seemings in some studies concerning some cases, and
these effects carry over to judgments based on the intuitions, it is
not the case that we should assign a large probability of error to
<em>all</em> intuitively based ethical beliefs. For one, we do not
have enough evidence to generalize from the cases where the effects
have been shown to say that <em>all</em> intuitions are subject to
these effects. For another, we are aware of these effects in a number
of other domains, but we do not conclude in those domains that all
judgments in those domains are unreliable (cf. Horne &amp; Livengood
2017). So though the framing and ordering effects are interesting and
we should take them into consideration when improving our moral
outlooks, it is not clear that they support the conclusion that moral
intuitions as a class are unreliable.</p>

<p>
Fourth, these experiments are trying to test intuitions about our
overall moral duties&mdash;that is, about what we should, or may do in
certain circumstances. As we have seen, not all intuitionists claim
that principles about what we ought to do are intuitive. W. D. Ross
for example claimed that only principles of <em>prima facie</em> duty
are self-evident, and principles of <em>prima facie</em> duty are,
roughly, principles stating that certain facts count in favour of an
act and others count against. So these principles state, for instance,
that the fact that one&rsquo;s act would produce some good, or the
fact that it would be the keeping of a promise, or the expression of
gratitude, etc., counts in favour of it, and the fact that, for
example, it would involve the infliction of harm on someone counts
against it. For one study on the stability of intuitions on the moral
valence (counting against or counting in favor) of Rossian-type
considerations, see James Andow (2018).</p>

<h3 id="DebuCertViewNormEthi">7.3 Debunking Certain Views in Normative Ethics</h3>

<p>
Other studies have shown emotional influence on moral judgment. Joshua
Greene, for instance, uses fMRI technology to look at what areas of
the brain are most active when subjects report moral judgments
(Greene, Sommerville, et al. 2001; Greene 2008). He finds that regions
of the brain associated with more emotional, &ldquo;intuitive&rdquo;
processing are more active in subjects who report characteristically
deontological judgments&mdash;for example, the intuition that it is
impermissible to push the fat man off the bridge in the footbridge
version of the trolley case&mdash;whereas regions of the brain
associated with deliberative cognition are more active in subjects who
report consequentialism-friendly judgments. (Because some emotional
areas of the brain are activated for subjects who give
consequentialism-friendly responses, Greene now says that the
deontological intuitions are more alarm-like, whereas the
consequentialist intuitions are more currency-like (Greene 2008: 41).)
Moreover, Greene reports that cognitive load, which is the burden of
being taxed with tasks that interfere with deliberative cognition,
selectively interferes with consequentialism-friendly responses. So
subjects who give the consequentialism-friendly response to Footbridge
while under cognitive load show longer response times (Greene,
Morelli, et al. 2008).</p>

<p>
Both Joshua Greene and Peter Singer have used results like these to
argue that moral intuitions, or at least deontology-friendly
intuitions, are likely unreliable (Greene 2008; Singer 2005). Why
unreliable? First, deontological intuitions are sensitive to
<em>morally</em> <em>irrelevant</em> factors, such as whether you kill
someone by pushing him versus throwing a switch. Second, deontological
intuitions are the result of fast and frugal &ldquo;intuitive
processes&rdquo; that were selected by evolution to produce fitness
enhancing behaviors in some environment, rather than to track the
moral facts (which perhaps this helps to explain the first thread of
reasoning; see also Timmons 2008).</p>

<p>
What are we to make of these results and the conclusions drawn by the
researchers? First, it is not clear whether intuitions as
characterized by Audi, Shafer-Landau, Huemer and others are elicited
in these studies, though this can be obfuscated by the fact that
psychologists call the fast and frugal processes responsible for some
moral judgments &ldquo;intuitive processes&rdquo;. Haidt and
Bjorklund, for example, define a moral intuition as &ldquo;the sudden
appearance in consciousness or at the fringe of consciousness, of an
evaluative feeling (like-dislike, good-bad)&hellip;&rdquo; (2007:
188). That does not sound like intuitions as characterized by
intuitionists themselves. We should try to make sure that the
propositions or mental states to which intuitional epistemologists
attribute epistemic roles are the ones under study before we draw any
negative conclusions about the justificatory role of intuitions from
these empirical results.</p>

<p>
Supposing that these studies do elicit intuitions as intuitional
epistemologists have understood them, still, any claim like
Greene&rsquo;s and Singer&rsquo;s that deontology-friendly intuitions
are sensitive to <em>morally irrelevant factors</em>, or are the
product of something selected by evolution <em>without regard to the
facts</em>, stakes some claim on what is and what is not morally
relevant, or what the moral facts are. These commitments are not the
result of empirical inquiry, but intuition. So even if some intuitive
justification is defeated when one appreciates these findings, it is
in part because intuition succeeds in justifying beliefs about what is
or is not morally relevant, or beliefs about the moral facts. Also,
the status of being a defeater is something the empirical results do
not deliver&mdash;at best they exploit the fact that we treat certain
considerations as defeaters (Berker 2009).</p>

<h3 id="DebuMeta">7.4 Debunking and Metaethics</h3>

<p>
The concerns just mentioned target deontological intuitions. Other
concerns are in a way broader, targeting general metaethical theories
that mix an intuitive epistemology with metaphysical doctrines about
the corresponding moral facts.</p>

<p>
The most influential criticism along these lines is the evolutionary
debunking argument. Sharon Street (2006) has pressed an evolutionary
debunking argument against moral realism (but see also Vavova 2015).
She argues that we have many of our moral judgments (or tendencies
toward certain moral judgments) because they are adaptive, and not
because they are mind-independently true. More generally, we might
wonder if any of the causal forces that make us have the intuitions
that we do have anything to do with the moral facts, particularly if
we understand those facts to be non-natural or otherwise <em>sui
generis</em> (Bedke 2009; 2014).</p>

<p>
These points are meant to be debunking in some way, but it is not
clear how to articulate the debunking premise. It could concern
modality&mdash;our intuitions or beliefs or the methods we use are not
safe or sensitive or modally reliable. It could concern
probability&mdash;our intuitions or beliefs are not likely true, or
are not likely to track truth. It could concern explanation&mdash;the
target facts are not explanatorily connected to why we have our
intuitions or beliefs. Whether some concern in this ballpark is
genuinely debunking remains hotly contested.</p>

<p>
If our moral metaphysics is non-naturalist, perhaps moral facts and
properties are non-causal. In that case, the debunking premise could
say we cannot know of something that is causally impotent. This would
be an application of a more general debunking argument against
causally impotent facts or properties. Applied to mathematics, for
example, it has been known as the Benacerraf-Field objection.
Intuitionists might respond by asking why a property must be causally
efficacious if we are to be able to know which things have that
property. Causal theories of knowledge have been largely rejected.
Alternatively, intuitionists could back off any commitment to
non-naturalism. That does not appear to be an obligatory metaphysical
position to pair with an intuitional epistemology (Bengson 2013).</p>

<p>
Debunking arguments like these are a topic unto themselves, and they
can be applied regardless of whether one&rsquo;s moral epistemology is
intuitional or not. That is to say, they are not a special problem for
intuitional moral epistemology.</p>

<h3 id="NonInfeJust">7.5 Non-Inferential Justification?</h3>

<p>
Intuitive justification is meant to be non-inferential. It is not
entirely clear what an inference is. But the basic idea is that a
mental state M2 is inferentially justified if it there is some
movement of mind from a distinct, justified mental state M1 to M2
whereby the justification of M1 is transmitted to M2. Intuitive
justification is non-inferential because intuitions are not the sorts
of things that are justified or not. They are unjustified justifiers.
This could also rule out perceptual justification as inferential, for
perceptual states are arguably not the sorts of things that are
justified or not.</p>

<p>
Sinnott-Armstrong (2006a), however, challenges the existence of any
non-inferential justification for moral beliefs. First, he argues that
the following considerations should lead one to mistrust intuitively
justified judgments:</p>

<ol type="a">

<li>which moral view one adopts affects one&rsquo;s self-interests, so
one is likely going to be biased when adopting a moral view;</li>

<li>there is often moral disagreement without reason to think one
party is more likely to be right than the other;</li>

<li>emotions influence one&rsquo;s moral view, and emotions cloud
judgment;</li>

<li>moral views are likely to be formed in circumstances conducive to
illusion, and shown by heuristics and framing effects; and</li>

<li>moral beliefs might be the product of morally suspicious sources,
e.g., if they are the result of the influences of the rich and
powerful.</li>
</ol>

<p>
He does not deny that some moral intuitions can justify moral beliefs.
But given that the defeasible justification they provide is undermined
by distorting factors, we need to check that any given moral intuition
is not one of the undermined ones, and we must seek corroboration of
their contents, before we can take it to provide justification. For
him, the beliefs that are justified by this process are inferentially
justified. If he is right, then those who have appreciated the
influences mentioned in (i)&ndash;(v) have at best inferentially
justified moral beliefs. They do not have non-inferential
justification.</p>

<p>
Nathan Ballantyne and Joshua C. Thurow (2013) disagree on this last
point. Couched in terms of undercutting defeaters, they say the
distorting factors that Sinnott-Armstrong mentions are offered as
undercutting defeaters of the justification of most of our moral
beliefs. One way to reinstate justification, they point out, is to
defeat the defeater. That reinstates the initial, non-inferential
support provided by the intuitions. Though we could get inferential
corroboration of our intuitively justified beliefs, it is also
possible that we simply assure ourselves that (i)&ndash;(v) do not
apply to a given intuition, in which case that intuition can carry on
as an unjustified justifier.</p>
